Segfault in TileImageResponse::cancel calling m_reply->deleteLater. m_reply likely dangling? Or response lifetime. In cancel:
```
if (m_reply) { m_reply->abort(); m_reply->deleteLater(); m_reply=nullptr; }
```
Could be called from a different thread? Thread is QQuickPixmapRea same likely. m_reply valid pointer maybe already deleted by network? If reply finished handler did `m_reply->deleteLater(); m_reply=nullptr;` before emitting finished. But cancel shouldn't see. Unless m_done? Hmm.

In cancel, m_reply points to deleted QObject (network reply was deleted when QNetworkAccessManager destroyed?). QNAM is child of response, not deleted. Hmm.

Maybe cancel is called during response destruction after our QNetworkAccessManager child destroyed? Order: QQuickImageResponse destructor deletes children. cancel called by framework before delete. m_reply may have been deleted due to QNAM? We never delete m_network except as child. Could be the reply was auto-deleted on error? QNetworkAccessManager replies are not auto-deleted. Maybe after `m_reply->deleteLater()` in finishWithError, deleteLater executes, but m_reply set nullptr. no.

Wait cancel may be invoked while a previous cancel already ran? m_reply null. Hmm.

Could be thread issue: response was created on one QQuickPixmapReader thread, cancel called from another thread after provider destroyed? deleteLater itself segfault implies object invalid (m_reply pointer dangling) or thread? QObject::deleteLater should be thread-safe. Likely dangling m_reply.

We should simplify: In cancel, just abort reply if pointer, do not deleteLater (QNAM owns? actually reply parent is QNAM, and response owns QNAM). If response gets deleted, QNAM child deletes reply. So remove `m_reply->deleteLater()` from cancel. Similarly in finishWithError and finished we call `m_reply->deleteLater()` then set null; okay. But on cancel, then response may be deleted and QNAM child deletes reply. Could double delete if deleteLater pending and QNAM deletes; Qt removes? yes.

Also in finished handler:
```
m_reply->deleteLater(); m_reply=nullptr; emit finished();
```
Then framework may call cancel? It might on finished? Could call response->cancel after finished? m_reply null. fine.
But if cancel after reply already deleteLater but before event loop, m_reply still non-null? We set null immediately. fine.

Maybe segfault because cancel called after response object was deleted? Not likely framework.

Let's remove deleteLater in cancel; just abort and set null. Also maybe the QNetworkReply finished lambda doesn't guard m_reply being different. If cancel aborts, reply emits finished; lambda captures this; m_reply is now null (set), but lambda uses `m_reply->...` without checking -> could dereference null. Wait current cancel sets m_reply=nullptr; abort causes finished synchronously? QNetworkReply::abort may emit finished immediately or queued. In lambda, `if (m_done) return; if (m_reply->error()...)` -> m_reply null => crash likely in finished not cancel. But gdb shows cancel line 39 deleteLater. Hmm line 39 maybe `m_reply->deleteLater` inside cancel after abort. If abort emitted finished synchronously, lambda set m_reply null and deleted, then cancel continues `m_reply->deleteLater()` with m_reply now null? That would be null deref at deleteLater (QObject::deleteLater on null -> segfault). Yes! Because we don't re-check after abort. `m_reply->abort()` synchronously emits finished, lambda sets m_reply=nullptr; then cancel's next line uses m_reply (null). Actually cancel code:
```
if (m_reply) {
  m_reply->abort();
  m_reply->deleteLater();
  m_reply=nullptr;
}
```
The member m_reply can change during abort; dangling/null. Solution: capture pointer local, disconnect connections before abort, set member null first:
```
QNetworkReply *reply = m_reply;
m_reply = nullptr;
m_cancelled = true;
if (reply) {
  reply->disconnect(this); // but connection context is reply itself, not this...
  reply->abort();
  reply->deleteLater();
}
```
Connections are `connect(m_reply, &finished, m_reply, [this])` context is m_reply, so `disconnect(reply, nullptr, this, nullptr)` won't work because receiver context is reply, not this. Change connect context to `this` for proper lifecycle. But response lives in same thread. Use `QObject::connect(m_reply, &QNetworkReply::finished, this, [this]...)`. Then cancel can `disconnect(m_reply, nullptr, this, nullptr)`, set m_reply null, abort, deleteLater. Also finished lambda guard.

Actually use `m_reply->disconnect(this)`? Connect receiver this; `disconnect(m_reply, nullptr, this, nullptr)`. Then abort. Good.
Simplify cancel:
```
m_cancelled = true;
QNetworkReply *reply = m_reply;
m_reply = nullptr;
if (reply) {
  QObject::disconnect(reply, nullptr, this, nullptr);
  reply->abort();
  reply->deleteLater();
}
```
But QNetworkReply might be owned by QNAM and double delete after? QNAM deletes children when response destroyed; deleteLater on reply while parent still exists; Qt handles removing from parent when deleted. fine.
Finished lambda:
```
if (m_done) return;
QNetworkReply *reply = m_reply; m_reply=nullptr;
if (!reply) return;
...
reply->deleteLater();
```
Set m_reply null at start, use local. On errors. This prevents reentrancy. finishWithError shouldn't touch m_reply since already null/local; can remove reply cleanup there.
Also `m_network` QNAM per response; after reply finished, fine.
Cancel may be called after finish: m_reply null.
Need check if response->cancel called from different thread than response affinity? QQuick pixmap reader cancels on its thread likely. fine.

Let's patch TileResponse robustly:
- connect with this context.
- finished handler copy reply local, set m_reply null, check done/cancelled.
- finishWithError only set error, emit if not done/cancelled.
- cancel disconnect/abort/deleteLater local.
Also deleteLater on reply in cancel and QNAM child; QNAM might later try delete its child list okay.

Let's patch.

